title the row closed twice -- a ledger that could not read its own statuses, and the meter that now does
prompt the standing seat prompt: roster cold then hot, reds first as standfast, QA on touch, read scope, and this lap's own log born on its day's shelf
think Reds-first picked the work rather than a plan. The pin carried four rows reading OPEN; three belong to other bodies (%304 constel is Dream's, %306 is Pond's, %311's ratchets are Sound's language custody), so %307 was the one left. Its open clause read "no guard reads an instruction file against the gate list" -- and tools/cu/custody_gate_instruction_witness.rish was already written, already rostered at tier lap, and GREEN on metal when I ran it: 27 instruction files, 7 gated mentions, 7 refusal-framed, bare_instructions=0, 11 of 11 control cases.
think The closure existed and stood in the wrong place. %308 wrote it in its own row, then folded to construction/archive/ the same day; commit 660b36a22's body says construction/REDS.md "closes %307's open clause with it", and git show of that commit's own REDS.md proves the edit never landed. So the record of the closure sat on a shelf a lap never walks while the stale marker stayed on the page reds-first reads first. That split is structural rather than accidental: every fold moves a closing row into a closed stack and leaves the row it closed on the open shelf.
think Two second looks, both taken in public because the first look was wrong. The first marker reading matched the literal strings **OPEN** and **CLOSED** and reported open_rows=3; the ledger writes eight spellings of those two words, including **OPEN, gated** on three rows, so a status meter was lying quietly. reds_fold.sh's looser bare-word test -- the whole uppercase word anywhere in a row -- already stood in the tree and read truer. The marker now reads bold text beginning with either word, last one on the line, and open_rows reads 6.
think Where the two readings still differ the scan reports rather than choosing a winner: fold_blocked_rows counts rows this reading does not call open whose text carries the bare word the fold tool refuses. It paid for itself on its first run -- three of the five it named were %291, %295 and %301, the very pin rows I had reached for to make room, each genuinely **OPEN, gated** on Keaton's word. Without that line the fold would have refused at row_open and the reason would have cost a lap to find.
obs The awk cost two real bugs before it read true, both mine and both caught by running it. An inner match clobbered RSTART and RLENGTH from the outer one, so the outer loop never advanced and the first run hung; and a duplicate row line on a second shelf overwrote a status a real row line had written, which is how closed_rows read 2 where three rows carry the marker. The control caught a third: a file left in the pen by the case before was a second ledger the scan read, so the table-row case passed for the wrong reason.
obs The rota read row 3, Water/Venus, care and flow, sense of taste: the threshold plus the shape-you-grew cardinal, the happy-zone fixed, growing-a-language dual. N is git rev-list --count HEAD at 3608 and 3608 mod 5 is 3; the previous Silence lap read row 2, so a clean advance and no hand-advance. The cardinal named this lap's fault exactly: Conway's law says a system copies the communication structure of whoever builds it, and a closure written where the writer stood rather than where the reader stands is a message that never arrives -- which is what a rotating population of agents sharing no memory does to a ledger. The fixed seat shaped the proof: the pen is the happy zone, 32 isolated cases that answer instantly, and the witness's three living-tree readings are the thin edge where the real ledger's own water enters. The dual seat governed the reuse -- diverge by strengthening rather than rewriting, so the spine file set and the row shape came from the scans that already spell them, and where this reading disagreed with reds_fold.sh I reported the difference rather than overriding the elder.
obs Two guards answered red on the cold roster and neither is mine to close. declared_ceiling reads law_copies_reciting=8 against a ceiling of 6 -- the card names it Mind's, and none of this lap's five new files touches the bound. remember_git_nib reads the card's nib stale, which this round's own amend pins. The cold run also reports tree_moved=yes: the peer pushed twice while it ran, so the hot run after git add is the reading that measures what ships.
obs Peer named at the open per REDS %291: grain-38, Sound's bench, mid-send on the chatgpt-mind lane. Its six modified files and its own untracked log were left untouched and the commit is path-limited around them.
